---
// promptanddone.com/ponytail: the PONYTAIL pack (DM deliverable for the "ponytail-lazy-senior-dev" reel).
import PackLayout from '../layouts/PackLayout.astro';
const install = '/plugin marketplace add DietrichGebert/ponytail\n/plugin install ponytail@ponytail';
const prompts = [
  'Add a date picker to the booking form so owners can choose the appointment date. Past dates shouldn\'t be allowed.',
  'Add a dark mode toggle to the app.',
  'Add form validation to the booking form with helpful error messages.',
  'Let owners upload a photo of their pet with the booking.',
];
const rows = [['Dark mode', '63, 67, 63', '21, 22, 20'], ['Validation', '109, 159, 157', '34, 30, 32'], ['Date picker', '59, 49, 58', '6, 6, 5'], ['Photo upload', '73, 124, 65', '42, 15, 10'], ['Total', '1,046', '243']];
const ponyDiff = "const [date, setDate] = useState('');\n// en-CA formats as YYYY-MM-DD in the owner's local timezone (toISOString would use UTC)\nconst today = new Date().toLocaleDateString('en-CA');\nawait createBooking({ owner, pet, phone, date });\n<Input label=\"Appointment date\" type=\"date\" min={today} value={date} onChange={(e) => setDate(e.target.value)} required />\nexport type Booking = { owner: string; pet: string; phone: string; date: string };";
---
<PackLayout title="Ponytail: the install + our 24-run test — Prompt &amp; Done">
  <main>
    <header>
      <p class="kicker"><a href="/">Prompt &amp; Done</a> · PONYTAIL pack</p>
      <h1>Same prompt, same Claude: 59 lines or 6.</h1>
      <p class="tag"><a href="https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail">Ponytail</a> is a free, MIT-licensed skill that makes your coding agent "think like the laziest senior dev in the room": before writing code it checks whether the thing already exists in your codebase, the standard library or the platform.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">1</span> Install (Claude Code)</h2>
      <p>Type these as two separate prompts in Claude Code:</p>
      <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
      <p>It is on every session after that. <code>/ponytail lite|full|ultra|off</code> changes the level. It also works with Codex, Cursor, Copilot and others: see the repo's INSTALL.md. Only install it from <code>DietrichGebert/ponytail</code>.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">2</span> Our test (20 Oct 2026)</h2>
      <p>A small React + TypeScript booking-form app with its own <code>Input</code> component. Claude Code, Opus 5.5, the same prompt with and without Ponytail, three runs each. We counted lines added in <code>git diff</code>.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Plain Claude</th><th>With Ponytail</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(([a, b, c]) => <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
      <p>All 24 versions passed the TypeScript check and <code>vite build</code>. Both date pickers block past dates in Chromium. Ponytail's runs also finished faster (323 s vs 465 s in total).</p>
      <p>The 6-line date picker it wrote reuses the app's own <code>Input</code> and the browser's built-in date picker:</p>
      <pre><code>{ponyDiff}</code></pre></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">3</span> The four prompts</h2>
      <ol>{prompts.map(p => <li><code>{p}</code></li>)}</ol></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">4</span> Fair to say</h2>
      <p>Less code is not automatically better code: plain Claude wrote more tests (in 7 of 12 runs vs 4 of 12). This is one small app and one model, so your numbers will differ. Ponytail's own benchmark (39 tasks, Opus 5.5) reports 53% less code.</p></section>
  </main>
</PackLayout>
